   316	\section{Parameters and definitions}\label{sec:app-params}\label{app:params}
   317	First test and headline market: providers $i = 1, \dots, 10$ have $q^{\max}_i = (0.60, 0.65, \dots, 1.00, 1.00)$, $b_i = (0.40, 0.38, \dots, 0.22)$, $a_i = 0.02$, $k_i = 6$, $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}] = [0.6, 1.4]$, $\sigma = 0.05$, $\beta = 0.05$, $\rho = 0.2$. Fresh markets of the second test: $N$, $J$, $T$, $\sigma$, $\beta$, $\rho$, and $v_{\min}$ as at the headline; $q^{\max}$ ten sorted draws from $U[0.55, 1.0]$; $b_i$ falling linearly in the quality rank between $b_{\mathrm{hi}} \sim U[0.34, 0.46]$ and $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \sim U[0.18, 0.26]$ with noise $U[-0.02, 0.02]$; $a_i \sim U[0, 0.05]$; $k_i$ uniform in $\{4, \dots, 8\}$; $v_{\max} \sim U[1.2, 1.6]$. The focal provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus $S^*$ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.
   318	
   319	\section{Proofs}\label{sec:app-proofs}\label{app:proofs}
   320	\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:local}.} Shading saves $sg$. Each audited escrow is refunded with probability $d$ instead of $d_0$. Given $K$, independence of delivery noise gives the probabilities $1 - (1-d)^K$ and $1 - (1-d_0)^K$ of at least one failure; the continuation loss is incurred once. Taking expectations over $U$ gives the identity.
   321	
   322	\paragraph{Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}.} A fresh identity faces the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states, up to relabelling, as the surviving identity without a failure; immediate free replacement attains it, so $C_U = 0$.
   323	
   324	\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}.} R3's priority list is ordered by age group, newest first. Within a group, independent continuous audit keys make the selected subset uniform among the group's jobs; delivery noise is independent of selection.
   325	
   326	\paragraph{Theorem~\ref{thm:renewal}.} The spell length is geometric with mean $1/q_E$; since survival to round $t$ is determined by earlier rounds, the expected spell profit is $r_E/q_E$. Adding the recovery's rewards and entry charges and dividing by the expected cycle length gives $\bar\Pi$ by the renewal-reward theorem~\cite{vlasiou2014renewal}. A cycle's surplus is at most $(r_E - h)/q_E + \epsilon\,\mathbb{E}D$; dividing by $1/q_E + \mathbb{E}D$ and using $\mathbb{E}D \ge L$ gives the bound.
   327	
   328	\paragraph{Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal}.} With $C_U = 0$, $A'(0) = s b - \phi(\tau/\sigma)\,\sigma^{-1}\,\mathbb{E}\sum_{j \in U} M_j \ge s\,(b - v_{\max}\phi(\tau/\sigma)/\sigma)$, since $M_j \le v_{\max}$ and $K \le s$.
   329	
